      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Acts 1:6-14",
          "refKey": "Acts.1.6-Acts.1.14",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "ascension-and-reign",
              "holy-spirit",
              "mission-and-witness",
              "prayer-and-worship"
            ],
            "image": [
              "cloud"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "longing-and-waiting",
              "resolve-and-sending"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "The second course of St. Paul xvi-xix"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "His third, as far as Rom. xix-xxviii THE ACTS"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "But ye shall receive power — and shall be witnesses to me - That is, ye shall be empowered to witness my Gospel, both by your preaching and suffering."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "A Sabbath-day's journey — The Jews generally fix this to two thousand cubits, which is not a mile."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "They went up into the upper room — The upper rooms, so frequently mentioned in Scripture, were chambers in the highest part of the house, set apart by the Jews for private prayer. These, on account of their being so retired and convenient, the apostles now used for all the offices of religion. Matt. x, 2; Mark iii, 14; Luke vi, 13."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 14,
              "v_end": 14,
              "text": "His brethren -- his near kinsmen, who for some time did not believe; it seems not till near his death."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 68:1-10, 32-35",
          "refKey": "Ps.68.1-Ps.68.10",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "justice",
              "providence-and-care"
            ],
            "image": [
              "hand-and-arm",
              "rock-and-refuge"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "praise-and-exultation"
            ],
            "function": [
              "praise-opening"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Jah — Is an abbreviation of the name Jehovah, which the Heathens pronounced Jao. Before him - Before the ark where he is present, as David is said to dance before the Lord, upon this occasion, 2 Sam. vi, 14."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Habitation — In heaven."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "Rebellious — Those who rebel against God."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Wentest — In the cloudy pillar, as their captain leading them up out of Egypt."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Dropped — Poured down great showers, which accompanied those mighty thunders."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "Weary — Dry and thirsty, and parched with excessive heat, and ready to faint for want of rain, chap. lxiii, 1."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 68,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "Thy congregation — The people of Israel. It - This land for the use of thy people: which God did by designing it for them, and expelling the old inhabitants; by furnishing it with all sorts of provisions, and making it fruitful by his special blessing. Poor - Such thy really were, when God undertook the conduct of them into Canaan."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "1 Peter 4:12-14; 5:6-11",
          "refKey": "1Pet.4.12-1Pet.4.14",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "hope-and-promise",
              "providence-and-care",
              "suffering-and-endurance"
            ],
            "image": [
              "fire"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "comfort-and-consolation",
              "exhortation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 4,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "Wonder not at the burning which is among you — This is the literal meaning of the expression. It seems to include both martyrdom itself, which so frequently was by fire, and all the other sufferings joined with, or previous to, it; which is permitted by the wisdom of God for your trial. Be not surprised at this."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 4,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "But as ye partake of the sufferings of Christ — chap. 1, while ye suffer for his sake, rejoice in hope of more abundant glory. For the measure of glory answers the measure of suffering; and much more abundantly."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 4,
              "v_start": 14,
              "v_end": 14,
              "text": "If ye are reproached for Christ — Reproaches and cruel mockings were always one part of their sufferings. The Spirit of glory and of God resteth upon you - The same Spirit which was upon Christ, Luke iv, 18. He is here termed, the Spirit of glory, conquering all reproach and shame, and the Spirit of God, whose Son, Jesus Christ is. On their part he is blasphemed, but on your part he is glorified - That is, while they are blaspheming Christ, you glorify him in the midst of your sufferings, chap. 16."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "John 17:1-11",
          "refKey": "John.17.1-John.17.11",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "ascension-and-reign",
              "church-and-unity",
              "prayer-and-worship",
              "trinity"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "wonder-and-awe"
            ],
            "function": [
              "lament-intercession"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "For himself, ver. 1-5. John xvii, 1-5"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "For the apostles, ver. 6-19; John xvii, 6-19 and again, ver. 24- 26. John xvii, 24-26"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "For all believers, ver. 20-23. John xvii, 20-23 And"
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "For the world, ver. 21-23. John xvii, 21-23 In his prayer he comprises all he had said from 31, and seals, as it were, all he had hitherto done, beholding things past, present, and to come. This chapter contains the easiest words, and the deepest sense of any in all the Scripture: yet is here no incoherent rhapsody, but the whole is closely and exactly connected.\n\n1. Father - This simplicity of appellation highly became the only- begotten Son of God; to which a believer then makes the nearest approach, when he is fullest of love and humble confidence. The hour is come - The appointed time for it; glorify thy Son - The Son glorified the Father, both before and after his own glorification. When he speaks to the Father he does not style himself the Son of man.\n\n2. As thou hast given him power over all flesh - This answers to glorify thy Son. That he may give eternal life, &c.-This answers to that thy Son may glorify thee. To all whom thou hast given him - To all believers. This is a clear proof that Christ designed his sacrifice should avail for all: yea, that all flesh, every man, should partake of everlasting life. For as the Father had given him power over all flesh, so he gave himself a ransom for all.\n\n3. To know - By loving, holy faith, thee the only true God - The only cause and end of all things; not excluding the Son and the Holy Ghost, no more than the Father is excluded from being Lord, 1 Cor. viii, 6; but the false gods of the heathens; and Jesus Christ - As their prophet, priest, and king: this is life eternal - It is both the way to, and the essence of, everlasting happiness.\n\n4. I have finished the work - Thus have I glorified thee, laying the foundation of thy kingdom on earth."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "The glory which I had — He does not say received - He always had it, till he emptied himself of it in the days of his flesh."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "I have manifested thy name — All thy attributes; and in particular thy paternal relation to believers; to the men whom thou hast given me - The apostles, and so ver. 12. They were thine - By creation, and by descent from Abraham. And thou hast given them me - By giving them faith in what I have spoken. So ver. 9."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Now they know that all things — Which I have done and spoken, are of thee - And consequently right and true."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "They have received them — By faith."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "I pray not for the world — Not in these petitions, which are adapted to the state of believers only. (He prays for the world at ver. 21, 23, that they may believe - That they may know God hath sent him.) This no more proves that our Lord did not pray for the world, both before and afterward, than his praying for the apostles alone, ver. 6-19, proves that he did not pray for them also which shall believe through their word, ver. 20."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 10,
              "v_end": 10,
              "text": "All things that are mine are thine, and that are thine are mine — These are very high and strong expressions, too grand for any mere creature to use; as implying that all things whatsoever, inclusive of the Divine nature, perfections, and operations, are the common property of the Father and the Son. And this is the original ground of that peculiar property, which both the Father and the Son have in the persons who were given to Christ as Mediator; according to what is said in the close of the verse, of his being glorified by them; namely, believing in him, and so acknowledging his glory."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 17,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "Keep them through thy name — Thy power, mercy, wisdom, that they may be one - with us and with each other; one body, separate from the world: as we are - By resemblance to us, though not equality."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
